    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The floor is in, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**What it does**\n- Each sheet stores a floor with two decimals. It starts at 1.00 and is set from a new \"Floor\" field in the Settings form. Saving it writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=floor value=<floor>` to the audit file, followed by `ROLLUP`/`TOTALS` lines when live dues move.\n- A claim's live due (amount \u00d7 rate) is now at least the floor. That one rule covers everything the request lists:\n  - Settling a claim freezes the floored figure, and a statement binds it.\n  - Closing a period fixes a total built from floored figures.\n  - The clerk-limit check also uses the floored figure.\n- Settled, statement-bound and closed-period figures are stored, so a later floor change doesn't touch them. A claim with no rate stays \"n/a\".\n- The due is worked out in two places, the server (`RollupService.figure`) and the browser (`claimDue` in `claims.service.ts`). Both apply the floor, so claim dues, person totals, the sheet total and period totals all add up the floored figures.\n\n**Change to how Settings saves:** the form now sends only the fields the user actually edited, and the server leaves any field that isn't sent unchanged. Without this, saving the floor would also have logged a clerk-limit change. Because of it, \"no clerk limit\" is now sent as an empty string rather than null. The new database migration is `V19__sheet_floor.sql`.\n\n**A bug outside the floor that scenario 2 hit:** when a statement bound a claim whose figure was \"n/a\", the person's due-total showed 0.00 instead of n/a. The code treated any bound claim as having a fixed figure, even one bound to \"n/a\". I fixed it on both sides (`Claim.keepsFigure()` on the server, `fixed()` in the browser), so only a real settled or bound figure counts.",
