    "probe_text": "I didn't change any files. There is no import feature in the code. The closest thing is **quick-add** (`QuickAddService`), which I've covered below.\n\n## 1. Figures and the rules behind them\n\n### Figures shown for a person (`people.ts`)\n| testid | Value | Computed by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `person-claimed` | Sum of the person's counting claims' `amount` | Client: `ClaimsService.claimedCents` |\n| `person-due-total` | Sum of their counting claims' figures, or `n/a` | Client: `People.dueTotals` |\n| `person-statement-count` | How many statements were taken for them | Server: `PersonService.statements` |\n| `person-stated-total` / `person-statement-date` | The last statement's `dueTotal` (or `n/a`) and its `takenOn` date | Server: `PersonView` |\n| `person-retired` / `person-retired-from` | Their `retiredFrom` date | Server |\n| `person-hold` | Their `held` flag | Server |\n| `rollup-claimed-total` / `rollup-due-total` | Sheet totals | Client: `totalCents`, `dueTotal` |\n\n### Figures shown for a claim (`person-claims.ts`)\n`claim-amount`, `claim-date`, `claim-note`, `claim-status`, `claim-number`, `claim-opened-by`, `claim-stated` (yes/no), `claim-period` (the first period whose dates contain the claim's date) and `claim-due` (from `claimDue`, or `n/a`).\n\n### Figures shown for a period\n`period-due-total`: while the period is open, the client sums the figures of counting claims dated in it (`Periods.dueTotals`). Once closed, it shows the stored `closedTotal`. `period-closed` shows whether it is closed.\n\n### Rules\n\n**Which claims count**\n- `ClaimStatus.counts()`: only `OPEN` and `SETTLED` claims count. `DRAFT` and `VOID` claims don't.\n- `RollupService.counts(person, claim)` adds one exception: a **held** person's `OPEN` claims don't count. Their `SETTLED` claims still do. The client has its own copy of this rule (`ClaimsService.live`).\n- Retired people's claims keep counting in every total. Retired people are only hidden from the list unless `show-retired` is on.\n\n**How a claim's figure (\"due\") is worked out** (`RollupService.figure`, copied on the client as `claimDue`)\n1. If the claim has a settled `due`, that is its figure.\n2. Otherwise, if it is `stated`, its figure is `statedDue`, which may be null (shown as n/a).\n3. Otherwise it uses the rate in force on the **claim's date** for the person's **current grade**. That is the rate with the latest `fromDate` on or before the date; a rate with no `fromDate` applies from the beginning of time (`RateRepository.findInForce`, `Rate.appliesOn`).\n4. The figure is `amount \u00d7 pct / 100`, rounded to the cent **HALF_EVEN**, then raised to at least the sheet's `floor` (default 1.00). The client does the same rounding in BigInt cents (`rates.service.ts due()`).\n5. With no rate in force, the figure is null and shows as n/a.\n\n**A person's due-total** (`RollupService.snapshot`, `People.dueTotals`)\n- It is the sum of the figures of their counting claims; claims with no figure are skipped.\n- It shows **n/a** when their grade has no rate in force on the **sheet's `today`** and none of their counting claims `keepsFigure()` (a settled due, or a stated figure that isn't null).\n- The sheet's due-total leaves out people whose due-total is n/a.\n\n**Dates**\n- Every decision and default date uses `Sheet.today`, never the real clock. The one exception: a new sheet starts with `today` set to `LocalDate.now()`.\n- Changing `today` moves which people are n/a, and that change is written to the audit file as ROLLUP/TOTALS lines.\n- A claim's own date decides which rate it uses and which period it falls in.\n\n**Claim lifecycle** (`ClaimStatus.canBecome`)\n- DRAFT \u2192 OPEN \u2192 SETTLED, and DRAFT or OPEN \u2192 VOID. Nothing goes back to DRAFT.\n- Only a DRAFT can be edited (409 otherwise).\n- Opening a claim gives it `number = Sheet.takeClaimNumber()`, a per-sheet counter that is never reused, and sets `openedBy`. A voided claim keeps its number, although the client-side comment wrongly says it is null.\n\n**Frozen figures**\n- **Settling** a claim freezes `due = figure(...)` at that moment. If it was stated, that is its stated figure. If there was no rate, `due` stays null and the claim keeps following the rates.\n- **A statement** (`ClaimService.statement`):\n  - records `Statement(dueTotal, claims count, takenOn = today)`;\n  - calls `bind(figure)` on each counting claim and `unbind()` on the person's other claims;\n  - leaves bound figures unaffected by rates, the floor or grade changes until the next statement.\n- **Closing a period** freezes `closedTotal`: the sum of the figures of counting claims dated in it. Claims inside the period still show live figures afterwards. Only the period's total is frozen.\n- The floor never changes settled or bound figures.\n\n**Who may do what**\n- The user who opened a claim may not settle it (403). This applies even to a steward.\n- A user's `Role` is `CLERK` by default, or `STEWARD` (`SettingsService.roleOf`).\n- If the sheet has a `clerkLimit`, a clerk can't settle a claim whose figure is above it, or whose figure is null (`requireMaySettle`). A steward can settle any claim, and with no limit set anyone can.\n- Editing a person needs the version they loaded; otherwise it is refused with 412 (optimistic locking). Names must be unique, ignoring case, for people and for periods.\n\n**Closed periods** (`PeriodService.requireOpenOn`)\n- Adding a claim dated in a closed period is refused.\n- Editing a claim is refused if its old **or** new date is in one.\n- Any status change to a claim dated in one is refused.\n- Not blocked: statements, holds, rate changes, grade changes and floor changes. These can still move the live figures of claims inside a closed period.\n- Periods may not overlap, and the to-date can't be before the from-date. Closing an already-closed period is refused.\n\n**Retirement**\n- A claim dated strictly **after** `retiredFrom` is refused when added or edited (`Person.retiredOn`). Claims dated on that day are allowed.\n- Retiring someone writes no ROLLUP lines.\n\n**Hold**\n- Toggling hold makes the person's OPEN claims leave or rejoin every live total, the period totals and statements, and it writes ROLLUP lines.\n\n**Quick-add**\n- Finds the person by name, ignoring case, or adds them with grade A.\n- Adds a DRAFT claim dated `today` with an empty note.\n- All in one transaction: if the claim is refused, the new person isn't kept.\n\n**Audit**\n- Every figure-moving change takes a `snapshot` before and calls `recordChanges` after. That writes a ROLLUP line per person whose figures moved and a TOTALS line if the sheet totals moved.\n- Records are written only after the change commits (`recordOnCommit`).\n\n## 2. How hard it was to follow\n\nIt was moderately hard. The server keeps the core rules fairly well in one place: `RollupService` has `counts`, `figure` and `snapshot`, and `ClaimService` has the guards. But every figure on screen is actually **recomputed a second time in the Angular client**: `claimDue`, `live`, `dueTotals` and `Periods.dueTotals`, plus a separate rounding routine. The rules for freezing, hold, n/a and closed periods are spread across `ClaimService`, `PeriodService`, `PersonService`, the entities and three front-end files, so a new developer has to read both copies and keep them in sync by hand."
